      <link>https://muhammadowaistahir06-svg.github.io/software/drone-swarm/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      
      <guid>https://muhammadowaistahir06-svg.github.io/software/drone-swarm/</guid>
      <description>&lt;style&gt;&#xA;.article-content, .max-w-prose { max-width: 900px !important; }&#xA;.project-meta { font-family: monospace; color: #4ade80; margin-top: -10px; margin-bottom: 30px; border-bottom: 1px dashed #334155; padding-bottom: 20px; font-size: 0.9em; letter-spacing: 0.5px; }&#xA;.project-body { color: #cbd5e1; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 1.05em; }&#xA;.img-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 15px; margin: 40px 0; }&#xA;.img-full { grid-column: span 2; width: 100%; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155; background: #0f172a; }&#xA;.img-half { width: 100%; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155; background: #0f172a; }&#xA;.project-body ul { margin-top: 15px; margin-bottom: 30px; padding-left: 20px; }&#xA;.project-body li { margin-bottom: 20px; }&#xA;.project-body strong { color: #f8fafc; }&#xA;.project-body h3 { color: #4ade80; text-transform: uppercase; font-family: monospace; font-size: 1.2em; margin-top: 40px; border-bottom: 1px solid #334155; padding-bottom: 10px; }&#xA;&lt;/style&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;project-meta&#34;&gt;DECOUPLED ARCHITECTURE | MPI + CUDA&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;project-body&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2&gt;At some point, simulating one drone wasn&#39;t enough. So I tried simulating thousands. :}&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&#xA;This project started with a simple question: &lt;h4&gt;how do you make a computer keep up when hundreds or thousands of objects all need to move, calculate, and react at the same time?&lt;/h4&gt;A traditional sequential CPU quickly becomes the bottleneck when every drone needs continuous position updates, 3D kinematics, and collision checks. So instead of simply optimizing the code, I changed the way the computation itself was organized.&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
      
    </item>
    
